A data protection impact assessment (DPIA) is required before processing when the type of processing—especially using new technologies—and considering its nature, scope, context, and purposes, is likely to result in a high risk to the rights and freedoms of natural persons [1]. It is specifically required for:  
- a systematic and extensive evaluation of personal aspects based on automated processing, including profiling, where decisions produce legal effects or similarly significantly affect the person [1];  
- processing on a large scale of special categories of data or personal data relating to criminal convictions and offences [1];  
- systematic monitoring of a publicly accessible area on a large scale [1].  

The assessment must also contain at least a systematic description of the processing and purposes, an assessment of necessity and proportionality, an assessment of risks, and the envisaged measures to address those risks [3][4]. The controller must seek the advice of the data protection officer when carrying out the DPIA, where designated [1].  

The documents do not cover every possible sector-specific trigger beyond Article 35, but they do say that where Article 6(1)(c) or (e) processing already has a legal basis in Union or Member State law and a DPIA has already been carried out as part of a general impact assessment for that legal basis, paragraphs 1 to 7 do not apply unless Member States require it [2].

Deployers of high-risk AI systems must:

- take appropriate technical and organisational measures to use the system in accordance with the instructions for use [1];
- assign human oversight to natural persons with the necessary competence, training, authority, and support [1];
- ensure, where they control the input data, that it is relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose of the system [1][3];
- monitor the operation of the system on the basis of the instructions for use, and inform the provider where relevant [3];
- if they have reason to think use in accordance with the instructions may create a risk under Article 79(1), they must without undue delay inform the provider or distributor and the relevant market surveillance authority, and suspend use of the system [3];
- if they identify a serious incident, they must immediately inform the provider, then the importer or distributor and the relevant market surveillance authorities [3];
- before putting a high-risk AI system into service or using it at the workplace, employers must inform workers’ representatives and affected workers [4];
- public authorities and Union institutions, bodies, offices or agencies must comply with registration obligations, and must not use a system that is not registered in the EU database; they must inform the provider or distributor if it is not registered [4];
- where applicable, use the information provided under Article 13 to comply with data protection impact assessment obligations under GDPR Article 35 or Directive (EU) 2016/680 Article 27 [4];
- cooperate with the relevant competent authorities in any action they take to implement the Regulation [7].

On impact assessments, some deployers must perform a fundamental rights impact assessment before deploying certain high-risk AI systems: bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers of high-risk AI systems referred to in points 5(b) and (c) of Annex III, before first deployment of the systems referred to in Article 6(2), except those intended for the area in point 2 of Annex III [2][5]. That assessment must cover the listed items, including the deployer’s processes, intended period and frequency of use, affected persons and groups, likely risks, human oversight measures, and measures for when risks materialise [2][5]. It applies to first use, may rely on previous or provider assessments in similar cases, and must be updated if elements change or are no longer up to date [5]. After completing it, the deployer must notify the market surveillance authority of the results using the filled-out template, unless exempt under Article 46(1) [5]. If an existing DPIA already covers some obligations, the fundamental rights impact assessment must complement it [5].

The documents do not say anything about deadlines for these assessments beyond “prior to deploying” and “before the first use,” or about all possible deployer obligations outside the provisions quoted here [2][5].

Under DORA, the contract must clearly allocate the rights and obligations of the financial entity and the ICT third-party service provider, and it must be set out in writing in one written document that includes the service level agreements and is available in paper or another downloadable, durable, accessible format [1].

At a minimum, the contractual arrangements must include:  
- a clear and complete description of all functions and ICT services, including whether subcontracting of critical or important functions is permitted and on what conditions [1];  
- the locations where functions/services are provided and where data is processed or stored, plus advance notice of location changes [1];  
- provisions on availability, authenticity, integrity and confidentiality of data, including personal data [1];  
- access, recovery and return of personal and non-personal data in an easily accessible format if the provider becomes insolvent, is resolved, discontinues business, or the contract ends [2];  
- service level descriptions, including updates and revisions [2];  
- provider assistance to the financial entity at no additional cost, or at a cost determined ex-ante, when an ICT incident occurs [2];  
- cooperation with competent authorities and resolution authorities [2];  
- termination rights and minimum notice periods [2];  
- conditions for the provider’s participation in ICT security awareness programmes and digital operational resilience training [2].

For ICT services supporting critical or important functions, the contract must also include, in addition to the above:  
- full service level descriptions with precise quantitative and qualitative performance targets, to allow monitoring and corrective action when service levels are not met [3];  
- notice periods and reporting obligations, including notification of developments that could materially affect the provider’s ability to deliver the services [3];  
- requirements to implement and test business contingency plans and to maintain appropriate ICT security measures, tools and policies [3];  
- details on the scope, procedures, and frequency of inspections and audits [4];  
- exit strategies, including a mandatory adequate transition period so the provider continues services during transition and the financial entity can migrate to another provider or in-house solutions [4].

The documents also say that, when negotiating these arrangements, the parties shall consider standard contractual clauses developed by public authorities for specific services [4].

The documents do not say whether any other contractual clauses are required beyond these listed provisions.
